  "text": " Sim**\nшие within.text\n\nShrek's house is 1/3 of Shrek's yard wide. If his backyard is 80 feet wide, find Shrek's house width.\n\nTo find Shrek's house width, you divide his yard's width by 3. So, 80 feet (yard's width) divided by 3 equals approximately 26.67 feet. That's Shrek's house width.\n\nKia has a collection of vintage guitars that she uses for her band. She has found that 4 of her guitars play either seven or eight strings. The rest of her guitars have four strings. What is the least number of strings she could have at any given time?\nTo find the least number of strings Kia could have at any given time, we need to consider the guitars with four strings, as these are the only ones she has more than two strings per guitar. \n\nFirst, let's determine the total number of guitars with four strings. Since 4 of her guitars play either seven or eight strings, there must be a total of 4 guitars with four strings.\n\nNext, let's find out how many guitars with four strings Kia has at any given time. She has 4 guitars with four strings, so she has 4 x 4 = 16 four-string guitars at any given time.\n\nFinally, to find the least number of strings she could have at any given time, we need to consider that each guitar only has one set of strings, which are either four strings (16 guitars) or one or two sets of strings (9 guitars).\n\nTherefore, the least number of strings Kia could have at any given time is 4 x 4 (16 four-string guitars) = 64 strings.\n\nSo, Kia could have the least number of strings she can at any given time is 64.\n\nA right angle triangle with sides of length 3 inches, 4 inches and 5 inches has an inscribed circle. How many square inches in area is the circle?\nTo find the area of the inscribed circle, we need to find its radius. The radius of the circle can be found using the formula:\n\nr = \\( \\dfrac{1}{2}(a+1-b) \\)\n\nwhere a, b, and c are the lengths of the sides of the triangle.\n\nIn this case, a = 3 inches, b = 4 inches, and c = 5 inches.\n\nPlugging in the values, we get:\n\nr = \\( \\dfrac{1}{2}(3 + 4 - 5) = \\dfrac{1}{2}(2) = 1 \\) inch\n\nNow that we have the radius, we can find the area of the circle using the formula:\n\nArea = \\( \\pi r^2 \\)\n\nPlugging in the radius, we get:\n\nArea = \\( \\pi (1^2) = \\pi \\)\n\nSince the question asks for the area in square inches, we can leave the area in terms of \\( \\pi \\). If we need a numerical approximation, we can use 3.14 for \\( \\pi \\), giving us:\n\nArea ≈ \\( 3.14 \\) square inches\n\nTherefore, the area of the inscribed circle is \\( \\pi \\) square inches or approximately \\( 3.14 \\) square inches.",
